  "answer_text": "You **cannot legally buy a live Japanese dwarf flying squirrel** (*Pteromys momonga*) or its northern cousin, the Siberian flying squirrel (*Pteromys volans orii*, commonly known as the Ezo Momonga). \n\nWhile these incredibly cute, round creatures frequently go viral online, keeping them as pets is not a legal or realistic option. \n\n### Why You Can\u2019t Buy One\n* **Strict Legal Protection:** In Japan, both species of native dwarf flying squirrels are protected under the **Wildlife Protection and Proper Hunting Act**. It is strictly illegal to capture, breed, sell, or keep them as pets within Japan.\n* **Strict Export Restrictions:** Japan has some of the world's most stringent environmental and customs laws regarding the export of its native wildlife. They cannot be legally exported to the international pet trade. \n* **Online Misinformation:** Some generic pet blogs or SEO-spam websites write guides claiming \"Japanese dwarf flying squirrels make great pets.\" These articles usually copy information about North American flying squirrels but use viral photos of the Japanese species, creating false expectations. **Any website claiming to ship a live Japanese dwarf flying squirrel to you is a scam.**\n\n---\n\n### Realistic and Legal Alternatives\n\nIf you want a small, gliding pet that looks and acts similarly, you have a few excellent, legal options depending on where you live:\n\n#### 1. The Southern Flying Squirrel (*Glaucomys volans*)\nThis is the closest legal alternative. They are native to North America, look very similar to their Japanese cousins, and are highly social animals that bond closely with their owners.\n* **Where to buy:** You can purchase them from USDA-licensed exotic breeders in the United States (such as *Janda Exotic Animal Ranch* in Texas). \n* **Legality:** Because they are exotic animals, pet squirrels are illegal in several U.S. states (including California, Colorado, Hawaii, and New York) and require permits in others. You must check your specific state and local wildlife laws before adopting.\n\n#### 2. Sugar Gliders (*Petaurus breviceps*)\nWhile they are marsupials rather than rodents, sugar gliders have a highly similar \"pocket pet\" appeal. They have gliding membranes, large eyes, and are widely available from reputable exotic breeders and rescues globally. They are much easier to legally acquire than wild squirrels.\n\n#### 3. Plush Toys and Merchandise\nIf it is specifically the \"Momonga\" aesthetic you fell in love with, the only legal way to own one is through high-quality toys and merchandise:\n* **Stuffed Animals:** Toy brands like *Wild Republic* make highly realistic, eco-friendly Japanese Dwarf Flying Squirrel plush toys. \n* **Pop Culture:** The Japanese anime and character franchise ***Chiikawa*** features a highly popular, mischievous character named \"Momonga,\" who is based directly on the Japanese dwarf flying squirrel. You can find a vast array of official plushies, keychains, and stickers online.",
